<!--
The test report: one file per test process, everything the ingester needs for
tests.tests, tests.environments and tests.runs. See proposal.md section 5.

Written by torch/testing/_internal/test_report.py, a pytest plugin that
common_utils.run_tests and test/run_test.py register with "-p
torch.testing._internal.test_report" and name the directory with the report-dir
option (XML comments cannot spell the flag). Every report, whatever launched
it, is <repo>/test-reports/<launched file>/<launched file>-<random>.report.xml,
with the file's path separators as dots (test_torch, distributed.test_c10d_nccl,
cpp.test_api): a tree at the repository root, apart from the junit files under
test/test-reports. The upload action and the in-run uploader add it to the
job's test-reports zip. Nothing in the junit pipeline reads it: it has no
<testcase> element, and the stats uploader skips the suffix.

Attempts are appended as they finish. A file without the closing </report>
tag is a process that died; its attempts still count. run_test.py closes such
a file and adds the test that was running as a crashed or timed_out attempt.

This example is the real output for test/junit_xml_testdata/pytest_suite.py,
cut to one attempt per outcome.
-->

<!--
What: the process's run context.
  repo:                   GITHUB_REPOSITORY; tests.tests.repo, part of test identity.
  github_workflow_job_id: JOB_ID, which every test workflow exports;
                          tests.runs.github_workflow_job_id. Everything else
                          about the job is read from default.workflow_job.
Both are absent outside CI, and the ingester skips reports without them.
-->
<report repo="pytorch/pytorch" github_workflow_job_id="109621633955">

  <!--
  What: the tests.environments identity fields (section 4.1), in the DDL's
    column order, measured in the process; the ingester hashes them into env_id.
    Values are what the process saw, never CI names.
